# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit a real key.
# Command Code Provider API: https://api.commandcode.ai/docs/provider
# Get a key from https://commandcode.ai/studio/ (any plan except Go).
CMD_API_KEY=
LLM_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash
LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.commandcode.ai/provider/v1
LLM_TIMEOUT=60
# Optional: enforce zero data retention on every request (x-cmd-zdr header).
CMD_ZDR=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Day 4 — ModelGateway. Keys live here, not code. "Which model, in what
# order" is runtime config: LLM_PROVIDERS is the ordered fallback chain.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OPENAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com/v1
# Ordered list primary → fallback, whitespace allowed after commas.
LLM_PROVIDERS=openai:gpt-4o-mini,anthropic:claude-3-5-haiku-latest

# Retry / timeout knobs (gateway-wide).
LLM_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3       # retries per provider, per call
LLM_TIMEOUT_S=60         # per-request socket timeout
LLM_BASE_DELAY_S=1.0     # first backoff; grows exponentially
LLM_MAX_DELAY_S=30.0     # backoff cap; Retry-After is always honored first
LLM_TOTAL_BUDGET_S=0     # seconds for the whole chain; 0 = unlimited

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Day 6 — RAG. Embeddings come from the OpenAI provider (Anthropic has none);
# the gate proceeds to the first provider whose Protocol has `embed`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
RAG_STORE_PATH=data/store.json
# Build the store from markdown:  python tools/build_rag_store.py --corpus corpus

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Day 6 §7 — Cloudflare Workers AI embeddings (optional). A cfut_* API token
# plus the account hex id from dash.cloudflare.com. Put `cloudflare:` first in
# LLM_PROVIDERS and it becomes the embed provider; it has no chat, so the
# gateway falls through to a chat-capable provider for generation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY=cfut_...
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=9c9f0c2d...
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=@cf/baai/bge-base-en-v1.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Day 14 — the read-only Saleor Data Agent. `SALEOR_READONLY_DB_URL` points at
# the `saleor_read_only` role the platform already provisions in
# saleor-platform/replica_user.sql (SELECT-only grants). Unset → the offline
# seeded sqlite stub (what every MECH check runs on; no driver required).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SALEOR_READONLY_DB_URL=postgres://saleor_read_only:saleor@localhost:5432/saleor
SALEOR_SQL_ROW_LIMIT=100        # rows per query; truncation is reported
SALEOR_SQL_SCHEMA_BUDGET=4000   # chars of schema card (Day 6's budget reflex, on a schema)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Day 17 — the agent state/checkpoint store. File-backed JSON; unset path → no
# persistence on disk (state off ≡ Days 12–16). Default TTLs are deliberately
# short — a long TTL is a conscious config, not memory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STATE_STORE_PATH=data/state.json   # where working/conversation/task state lives
STATE_DEFAULT_TTL=86400         # seconds a record lives when a write inherits
STATE_MAX_RECORDS=1000          # store cap; oldest-first eviction
STATE_PRINCIPAL=default         # owner used when a run names none

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Day 26 — the permission layer. `PERMISSIONS_PATH` is the grant file (who may
# call what, and what may come back), read beside Day 15's policy.yaml; unset it
# and the shipped permissions.yaml at the repo root is the default. `PERMISSIONS`
# is the single switch: `none` restores the ungoverned path (no check, no result
# contract, no audit) — the C9 control, which proves the layer is *passive* by
# showing a legitimate request is byte-identical with it on and off. The layer is
# authorization, not authentication: `AGENT_PRINCIPAL` is a caller-supplied
# string (beside STATE_PRINCIPAL), and Day 29 owns who is actually asking.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PERMISSIONS_PATH=permissions.yaml
PERMISSIONS=on                  # `none` restores the ungoverned path (the C9 control)
AGENT_PRINCIPAL=default         # the principal a governed run acts as; unknown → default
AUDIT_LOG_PATH=data/audit.jsonl # append-only, hash-chained decision log (no values)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Day 27 — human-in-the-loop. An approval is a durable record in Day 17's state
# store (`kind=approval`, author "approver"), opened by the permission layer when a
# grant declares `confirm: human` and resolved out of band by a person. `APPROVALS`
# is the single switch: `none` removes the gate entirely, so a `confirm: human`
# grant opens no request, no run stops, and no chain entry is written — the H9
# control, which proves the layer is *passive*. Note the two clocks, because they
# are deliberately different: `APPROVAL_TTL_S` is the **decision window** (an
# approval that never expires is a standing permission), while the store's own TTL
# (`STATE_DEFAULT_TTL`) is the **retention of the record** — a record that vanished
# at the window could not report that it expired. `APPROVAL_MAX_PER_RUN` is the one
# genuinely new resource bound: unlike the audit log, the thing creating requests is
# not only the model, so a caller could flood a decision queue by asking repeatedly
# (real rate limiting is Day 28's). `APPROVAL_EXECUTOR_PRINCIPAL` names the
# separately-granted principal that applies a staged effect; it is deliberately not
# the requester, so "who approved" and "who ran it" stay two answers.
#
# The surface is a CLI and two read-only routes on purpose: `server.py` has no
# authentication, so the decision route does not exist. See tools/approvals.py.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APPROVALS_PATH=data/approvals.json  # the approval shelf (a state-shaped store)
APPROVALS=on                    # `none` removes the gate (the H9 control)
# APPROVALS=none                # the H9 control spelled out: the gate does not exist,
#                               # so a `confirm: human` grant opens nothing at all
APPROVAL_TTL_S=900              # the decision window; a long TTL is a conscious config
APPROVAL_MAX_PER_RUN=1          # requests one run may open (queue flooding is Day 28's)
APPROVAL_EXECUTOR_PRINCIPAL=executor   # who may apply: a grant, never the requester
# PENDING (recorded, not fixed here): this file is already missing ~31 variables that
# code reads (RAG_CODE_STORE_PATH, TOOL_GATE_TAU, AGENT_MAX_STEPS + eight more,
# SALEOR_SQL_POLICY, AGENT_MAX_REPLANS, the six memory and six orchestration knobs,
# four evaluation knobs), and `CMD_ZDR` above is read by no code. The reference docs
# document them; only this copy-me template is stale. A security day is the wrong day
# to fix a documentation gap in the file that holds `CMD_API_KEY`.
